WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.370
- Well, they were so fed
up like I was so, you know,

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:07.040
I'm a small business guy
who, the previous governor,

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.277
Martin O'Malley had raised
taxes 43 times in a row.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:14.114
And it had crushed our economy.

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:17.951 align:start size:97% position:3%
We had lost 10,000
businesses and 100,000 jobs

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:21.755
and people were fleeing the
state in all directions.

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:23.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it was frustrating
to a lot of people.

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:25.525
And it was to me as a
small business owner.

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:28.561
And it didn't matter what
your party affiliation was,

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:32.265 align:start size:97% position:3%
people were leaving, and a
Gallup poll came out that said

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:35.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
47% of all Marylanders
wanted to leave the state.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:37.037
And that was kind of the straw

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:38.838 align:start size:84% position:16%
that broke the
camel's back for me.

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:42.308
(warm instrumental music)

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:49.682 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin, and this
is "The Rubin Report."

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.651 line:0%
As always guys, don't
forget to subscribe

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:54.854 line:0%
to our YouTube channel and
click that notification bell.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:58.892 align:start position:0% line:0%
And joining me today is the
62nd governor of the great state

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:01.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
of Maryland and the
author of "Still Standing:

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:04.964 align:start size:88% position:13%
Surviving, Cancer,
Riots, a Global Pandemic

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:08.601
and the Toxic Politics
that Divide America."

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:11.538 align:start size:97% position:3%
Governor Larry Hogan, welcome
to "The Rubin Report."

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:13.039
- Well, thank you, Dave.

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:15.141
It's really great to be with
you, thanks for having me.

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.211
- My pleasure, that is
quite a title of a book.

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:22.415
I mean, you have got, your
title is a book unto itself.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:23.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right.
- Which I thought would be

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:24.918
and interesting--
- Well, you made it

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:25.985
all the way through so I
was impressed with that.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:27.353
So thank you.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:30.457
- I've become a master
of the teleprompter,

00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:32.959
but actually I thought that
would be an interesting place

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:36.362
to start because the book
is coming out this week

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:40.767
and you actually made a whole
bunch of additions to the book

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.936 align:start size:84% position:16%
because of what's
happening right now.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:44.671 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I thought that
might be the right spot

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:46.439 align:start size:88% position:13%
for us to kick off here.
- Right, yeah.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:49.876 line:0%
- Dave, it was already a kind
of a long title to begin with

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:53.146 align:start position:0% line:0%
because we were trying to cover
a lot of important topics,

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:54.481
you know, and I was covering,

00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:58.218
when I was elected
kind of overcame,

00:01:58.218 --> 00:02:01.154 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was the biggest upset
in America in 2014

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:02.589
in a very blue state.

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:05.625 align:start position:0%
I was immediately hit with
dealing with riots in Baltimore

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:08.228 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then life-threatening
cancer and, you know,

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:10.730
we had lot of interesting
stories to write about.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.165
I really wanted to
talk a little bit,

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:13.633
it's not a heavy political read,

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I wanted to talk a
little bit about, you know,

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:18.605 align:start size:97% position:3%
the divisive politics today
and some of my thoughts

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:20.140
on where we ought to be heading.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:25.145
And we put together this
book sort of a February-ish

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:27.580
timeframe was when we
were finished with it.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.382 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it was going to come
out a little earlier

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:32.218
and then the pandemic,
we kind of put it on hold

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:34.220 align:start size:84% position:16%
and asked the
publisher to hold it.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.389 align:start size:81% position:19%
They would only
hold it until now.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.758
And they asked us
if we would add

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:41.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
some more current activities,
'cause I was dealing

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.531
with this crisis and we
added about five chapters

00:02:44.531 --> 00:02:46.032
on the pandemic and
I think it makes it

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:49.202 align:start size:88% position:13%
kinda more
interesting, and I tell

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.271
some pretty straightforward
stories about what we've

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:53.106 align:start size:81% position:19%
been dealing with,
but I'm hoping

00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:54.941
people will find it interesting.

00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:58.278
- Yeah, so I want to go into
each of the specific topics

00:02:58.278 --> 00:02:59.879
that you break down the title,

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:02.382
but let's start with
the pandemic stuff,

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:03.917
because as you just mentioned,

00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:07.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
you are a Republican governor
of a largely blue state.

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:10.089
If I'm not mistaken, only
two Republican governors

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
have ever been
reelected in Maryland,

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:14.594
in its entire history.

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:16.162 line:0%
So maybe we should
start there first.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:19.566 align:start position:0% line:0%
What's it like being an
outsider governor, whether it's,

00:03:19.566 --> 00:03:21.668 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
you know, a red
guy in a blue state

00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:23.136 line:0%
or a blue guy in a red state,

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:25.705 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
just what's that like
first before pandemic time

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:26.639
and then we'll talk about that.

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:28.808
- Sure, yeah, so I, you know,

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:31.311 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm a lifelong
small business guy.

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:34.847
Never held elective office
until I was elected governor.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:39.118 align:start position:0%
Our state was, it has the
highest democratic registration

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.486
of any state in America.

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:42.989
In 2014, I got elected.

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:44.357
It was kind of a shock.

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:46.092
I mean, nobody expected me to.

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:48.394
I came from nowhere
and we weren't even
on the radar screen.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:51.798 align:start position:0%
It was kind of, I was the least
likely to succeed, I think.

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:53.933
Nobody thought I was
going to be governor,

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:57.337
but I pulled together a
coalition of, you know,

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:59.572 align:start size:88% position:13%
all the few Republicans
we had in our state

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.174 align:start size:81% position:19%
along with a bunch
of independents

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:03.142
and Democrats and I got elected.

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:07.513
And then I'm governing in a
state that's a fairly liberal

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:12.051 align:start size:84% position:16%
Democratic state
with the legislature
that's 70%, you know,

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:14.053
Democrats in both chambers,

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:17.757 align:start size:97% position:3%
every single statewide elected
official is a Democrat.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm only the second Republican
50 years to be elected,

00:04:20.994 --> 00:04:24.097
and the second one to be
reelected in 242 years.

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:27.500
So it's look, I think one
of the things I've learned

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.170
in a situation like that is
that you've got to figure out

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:36.175
a way to work with people on
the other side of the aisle.

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:39.846
And it may be different if
you're, say, a Republican

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:43.950
in a red state or you're a
Democrat in a blue state,

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.486 align:start position:0%
you don't have to develop those
relationships and you don't

00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:50.156 align:start size:88% position:13%
have to maybe
compromise or find a way

00:04:50.156 --> 00:04:52.292 align:start size:84% position:16%
to reach that middle
ground, you know,

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:53.693
where you can all work together.

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:56.329 align:start size:94% position:6%
So maybe out of necessity
to get things done

00:04:56.329 --> 00:05:00.366
I had to be more pragmatic and
figure out, how do you reach

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:03.670
common sense solutions and
actually fix some problems?

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:06.873
And it turns out that's
what a lot of people seem

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:09.742
to want because you know, a lot
of Democrats and Republicans

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:13.313 align:start size:97% position:3%
and independents seemed to
like it and they reelected me.

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:16.449
And you know, we seem to
be making some progress.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:17.517
- Are you telling me that people

00:05:17.517 --> 00:05:19.719
vote you guys in
to fix problems?

00:05:19.719 --> 00:05:21.421
Is that what it's all about?

00:05:21.421 --> 00:05:25.291 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, I know, Dave, it
sounds like a strange concept

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:27.927
in politics today 'cause
we don't see a lot of that,

00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:30.797
but I really do think it's
what a lot of people want.

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:34.901
I mean, you know, we can
talk about the politics

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:36.402
on the right and the left.

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:39.605 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think most people,
regardless of where they fall

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:44.010
on the spectrum, they really
do want the people they elect

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.546
to office, regardless of
which side of the aisle

00:05:46.546 --> 00:05:47.780 align:start size:84% position:16%
they come from, they
just want people

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.516
to get to work and
get things done.

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:54.153 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think they're frustrated
with the political process

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:56.456
and the fact that they
see a lot of divisiveness

00:05:56.456 --> 00:05:57.857
and dysfunction, they
don't think people

00:05:57.857 --> 00:05:59.826
are getting the job done.

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:01.761
- You mentioned that you
were a small businessman

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:03.463
before going into politics.

00:06:03.463 --> 00:06:06.532
My sense is that the
future of politics,

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:09.569 align:start size:97% position:3%
probably on either side,
although I think probably more

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:11.871
on the Republican side is

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:15.408
that the day of the
longterm politician,

00:06:15.408 --> 00:06:19.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
the guy who comes up
through the system is over

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:21.114 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that the future
will be people

00:06:21.114 --> 00:06:23.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
who built their own
businesses, who innovated,

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:25.151
maybe were in the tech world,

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:27.653 align:start size:84% position:16%
that those will be the
future politicians.

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:31.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you think that gave you
unique a unique way of looking

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:35.428 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the world that has been
relevant for governing?

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:37.797
- You know, well, first
of all, I agree with you.

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:40.500 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that is what
we're gonna see.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.001
It's what we should see.

00:06:42.001 --> 00:06:45.338
I mean, I've, for a long
time sort of been arguing

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:48.074 align:start size:84% position:16%
against career
politicians who spend

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:51.711
their entire professional
lives in elective office.

00:06:51.711 --> 00:06:54.313
And I think it's what the
founding fathers originally

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:56.716
had in mind, you know,
about people just leaving

00:06:56.716 --> 00:06:59.385
whatever it was they normally
did and going in and serving

00:06:59.385 --> 00:07:00.887
for a while in public service.

00:07:02.021 --> 00:07:03.523 align:start size:84% position:16%
I actually, you
know, I'm the chair

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:05.291 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the National
Governors Association

00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:08.094 align:start size:97% position:3%
and active in the Republican
Governors Association.

00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:11.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
And a lot of my colleagues
have similar profiles

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.933 align:start size:88% position:13%
and backgrounds, not all
of them, many of them

00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:16.202
are not lifelong politicians.

00:07:16.202 --> 00:07:18.371 align:start size:84% position:16%
Many of them have
business backgrounds.

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:20.940
And I think it may be a
sort of a new phenomenon,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.075
but I'm not alone
in that regard.

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:26.078
And I think maybe in
the governor's races,

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:29.148
you may send somebody off to
Washington that's, you know,

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:33.453
been a politician, but when
you're deciding who the CEO

00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:36.355 align:start size:97% position:3%
or the chief executive is
going to be to run your state,

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:38.157
you know, they want
to pick somebody

00:07:38.157 --> 00:07:39.559 align:start size:81% position:19%
that's kind of
competent and able

00:07:39.559 --> 00:07:41.727
to run things and
get things done.

00:07:41.727 --> 00:07:44.330
And so you see, see a lot
of business backgrounds

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.499
in the folks that are
running the states.

00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:48.768 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, it's one--
- I think it's helped me a lot

00:07:48.768 --> 00:07:51.504
I mean, you know, I knew a
little bit about government

00:07:51.504 --> 00:07:53.539 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I knew some
stuff about politics,

00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:56.476 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the business background,
you know, my whole career

00:07:56.476 --> 00:07:58.277
was about trying to
bring people together

00:07:58.277 --> 00:08:00.913
to get things done,
and it turns out

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:04.784 align:start position:0%
that's actually a valuable thing
to have in government, too,

00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:06.819
(laughs) you know.
- You don't say.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.788 align:start size:88% position:13%
Accomplishing stuff.
- Yeah, I mean, strange.

00:08:08.788 --> 00:08:10.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
- And getting things done.
- Nobody ever

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:13.025
figured that out before.
- Do you find Maryland

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:15.995
to be a particularly
unique case these days

00:08:15.995 --> 00:08:19.198 align:start size:88% position:13%
because of the proximity
to Washington, D.C.

00:08:19.198 --> 00:08:22.869
where so many of the longtime
bureaucrats and the people

00:08:22.869 --> 00:08:24.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
that have just been
in the system forever,

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.073
a ton of them live in
Maryland, obviously.

00:08:27.073 --> 00:08:29.709 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that maybe
filters a little bit

00:08:29.709 --> 00:08:31.811
of what the political
life is like there.

00:08:32.778 --> 00:08:34.180
- Maybe to a certain extent.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:37.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, one of our
largest population centers

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:39.619
is in metropolitan Washington,

00:08:39.619 --> 00:08:41.320 align:start size:81% position:19%
but we're a pretty
diverse state.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.456 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, we've got the
Washington Metropolitan Area,

00:08:43.456 --> 00:08:46.192 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Baltimore Metropolitan
Area, but we've got, you know,

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:49.128
some pretty dense areas
we've got, you know,

00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:52.798
but we've got suburban areas,
very rural areas of our state,

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:55.201
about 70% of our state is rural.

00:08:55.201 --> 00:08:57.570
And so we're kind
of a microcosm.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.939 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, we're
America in miniature.

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:01.874 align:start position:0%
We have a little bit of the
same things that everybody has.

00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:03.309
We have probably a
higher percentage

00:09:03.309 --> 00:09:05.244
of federal government employees.

00:09:05.244 --> 00:09:06.712
So we actually have more people

00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:08.714 align:start size:97% position:3%
that are in the private sector
that maybe have some kind

00:09:08.714 --> 00:09:12.585 align:start position:0%
of government contracting
affiliation, but it may change.

00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:15.221 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're a little bit more
liberal and a little bit more

00:09:15.221 --> 00:09:18.190
Democratic leaning
as a result of that.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.359 align:start size:97% position:3%
It made it more challenging
for me to get elected,

00:09:20.359 --> 00:09:22.194
but you know, we've
been able to win over

00:09:22.194 --> 00:09:25.031
some of those folks,
which is common sense.

00:09:25.031 --> 00:09:27.366
- What was going on there
before you were elected

00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:32.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
that a blue state would say,
oh, let's let this guy in

00:09:32.104 --> 00:09:33.606
who's not a career politician,

00:09:33.606 --> 00:09:35.975
who's cobbling together this
kind of strange coalition.

00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:40.112
- Yeah, well, they were
so fed up, like I was.

00:09:40.112 --> 00:09:44.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I'm a small business
guy who, previous governor

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:48.654
Martin O'Malley had raised
taxes 43 times in a row.

00:09:48.654 --> 00:09:51.524
And it had crushed our economy.

00:09:51.524 --> 00:09:55.394 align:start size:97% position:3%
We had lost 10,000
businesses and 100,000 jobs

00:09:55.394 --> 00:09:59.198
and people were fleeing the
state in all directions.

00:09:59.198 --> 00:10:00.800 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it was frustrating
to a lot of people.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.935
And it was to me as a
small business owner.

00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:06.005
And it didn't matter what
your party affiliation was,

00:10:06.005 --> 00:10:07.540
people were leaving.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:11.777
A Gallup poll, came out that
said 47% of all Marylanders

00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:13.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
wanted to leave the state,
and that was kind of

00:10:13.846 --> 00:10:16.816 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
the straw that broke
the camel's back for me.

00:10:16.816 --> 00:10:20.486 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
We were 49th out of 50 States
in economic performance.

00:10:20.486 --> 00:10:24.890 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
So I was frustrated enough
to do something crazy,

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.593
like say, I've had enough,
I'm gonna run for governor.

00:10:27.593 --> 00:10:30.663
And it was the point where
Democrats said, you know,

00:10:30.663 --> 00:10:32.398
I'm a lifelong Democrat.

00:10:32.398 --> 00:10:35.434
I've never considered voting
for a Republican in my life,

00:10:35.434 --> 00:10:37.536 align:start size:88% position:13%
but this guy is
starting to make sense.

00:10:37.536 --> 00:10:41.240 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I focused on the economic
issues and talked about,

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:44.377
you know, we were gonna
cut taxes and regulations

00:10:44.377 --> 00:10:46.612
and focus on growing
our private sector

00:10:46.612 --> 00:10:48.381
and trying to put more
people back to work,

00:10:48.381 --> 00:10:49.849
and it's exactly what we did.

00:10:49.849 --> 00:10:51.450 align:start size:97% position:3%
We had the biggest economic
turnaround in America.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:55.688
We had more businesses open
and put more people to work

00:10:55.688 --> 00:10:57.456
than ever before in the
history of the state.

00:10:57.456 --> 00:11:00.359 align:start position:0%
And we got things done even
with our Democratic legislature.

00:11:00.359 --> 00:11:02.428
- Do you think bottom
line, when you get rid

00:11:02.428 --> 00:11:04.697
of all the other issues that
you're dealing with all day,

00:11:04.697 --> 00:11:07.533 align:start size:97% position:3%
that if you as a governor
can keep the economy chugging

00:11:07.533 --> 00:11:11.137
and keep people at work that
you've basically done your job?

00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:14.573
- Well, you know, we're
wrestling with all kinds

00:11:14.573 --> 00:11:17.076
of really tough issues, Dave.

00:11:17.076 --> 00:11:19.912 align:start size:94% position:6%
When you're governor of
the state, as I mentioned,

00:11:19.912 --> 00:11:21.614 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, we're
dealing with, you know,

00:11:21.614 --> 00:11:23.716
close to a $50 billion
budget and, you know,

00:11:23.716 --> 00:11:25.651
60 some thousand employees,

00:11:25.651 --> 00:11:27.420
we have to provide all
these different services.

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:28.888
There are so many
different issues,

00:11:28.888 --> 00:11:31.757 align:start size:97% position:3%
but really it is, we're
dealing with educating folks

00:11:31.757 --> 00:11:34.393 align:start position:0%
and handling transportation
and, you know, law enforcement,

00:11:34.393 --> 00:11:39.398
all these multitude of
things, but the focus

00:11:40.633 --> 00:11:41.767
that brought everybody
together in our state

00:11:41.767 --> 00:11:43.269
that they could agree on,

00:11:43.269 --> 00:11:46.572
they could argue about
various different issues.

00:11:46.572 --> 00:11:48.007
But the one thing
they all agreed on

00:11:48.007 --> 00:11:49.942
was the pocket book issues.

00:11:49.942 --> 00:11:51.210
And that's how I put together,

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:53.345
I put a group together
called Change Maryland.

00:11:53.345 --> 00:11:55.815
It was a nonpartisan
citizen organization.

00:11:55.815 --> 00:11:57.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we had just as many
Democrats and independents

00:11:57.383 --> 00:12:00.319 align:start size:84% position:16%
as Republicans, and
they were just saying,

00:12:00.319 --> 00:12:02.054
let's focus on these issues.

00:12:02.054 --> 00:12:04.123 align:start size:97% position:3%
Let's not argue about all
these other things right now.

00:12:04.123 --> 00:12:05.858
Let's just focus on these.

00:12:05.858 --> 00:12:07.626 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we brought
together a coalition.

00:12:07.626 --> 00:12:09.128
It turned out to be
a winning coalition.

00:12:09.128 --> 00:12:11.864 align:start size:97% position:3%
And, and you know, when
people say, how did you get,

00:12:11.864 --> 00:12:14.166 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, suburban women and
how do you get minorities

00:12:14.166 --> 00:12:15.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
and how do you get
Democrats and independents

00:12:15.901 --> 00:12:17.703
to vote for a Republican?

00:12:17.703 --> 00:12:20.306
But I think it's one of the
things that Republican party

00:12:20.306 --> 00:12:22.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
is gonna have to think
about, how do we reach out

00:12:22.241 --> 00:12:24.276
to a wider group of people

00:12:24.276 --> 00:12:26.011 align:start size:88% position:13%
in order to win
elections in the future

00:12:26.011 --> 00:12:28.748 align:start size:97% position:3%
so we can be in the position
of making decisions?

00:12:28.748 --> 00:12:30.816
- Yeah, so I want
to get more to that

00:12:30.816 --> 00:12:32.685 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the sort of pure
political side in a minute,

00:12:32.685 --> 00:12:34.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
but let's talk about
what's going on--

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:36.822 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I didn't mean to get too
political on you, Dave.

00:12:36.822 --> 00:12:38.591
- (laughs) A governor
getting political?

00:12:38.591 --> 00:12:40.126
I can't believe it.

00:12:40.126 --> 00:12:41.627
- Yeah, let's not
do that anymore.

00:12:41.627 --> 00:12:44.330 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, yeah, let's talk about
what's going on now though,

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:46.832
because you know, there's been,

00:12:46.832 --> 00:12:50.770 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think the major conversation
really at a high level

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:53.739
is sort of what the federal
government is supposed to do

00:12:53.739 --> 00:12:55.775
related to coronavirus

00:12:55.775 --> 00:13:00.379 align:start size:97% position:3%
and what the state governments
are supposed to do.

00:13:00.379 --> 00:13:02.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
I suspect I generally know
your thoughts on this,

00:13:02.982 --> 00:13:06.252 align:start size:97% position:3%
but what do you think your
job as a governor is right now

00:13:06.252 --> 00:13:07.787
and what is not your job?

00:13:07.787 --> 00:13:10.489 align:start size:84% position:16%
What is the job of the
federal government?

00:13:10.489 --> 00:13:11.924
- Well, that's a great question.

00:13:11.924 --> 00:13:14.460 align:start size:97% position:3%
Look, I think the governors
are on the front lines

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.462 align:start size:84% position:16%
of this crisis,
and we ought to be.

00:13:16.462 --> 00:13:20.166 align:start position:0%
We are out there, we're closest
to the people's problems.

00:13:20.166 --> 00:13:22.735 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're working together
with our local governments.

00:13:22.735 --> 00:13:24.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we're trying to really
address these issues

00:13:24.637 --> 00:13:26.605
that are on the ground
in our individual states.

00:13:26.605 --> 00:13:28.741 align:start size:84% position:16%
And each of our
states are different.

00:13:28.741 --> 00:13:30.409
We're going after, you know,

00:13:30.409 --> 00:13:33.546
the different situations on
the ground in our states,

00:13:33.546 --> 00:13:37.016 align:start size:97% position:3%
but there was a major, there
were certain big things

00:13:37.016 --> 00:13:39.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
that only the federal
government could do well.

00:13:39.118 --> 00:13:41.720
So, you know, early
on in this crisis,

00:13:43.155 --> 00:13:46.125 align:start size:97% position:3%
the federal government could
have taken more of a role

00:13:46.125 --> 00:13:48.327
in an overall testing strategy.

00:13:48.327 --> 00:13:50.262
They did, you know, they
were a little bit, I think,

00:13:50.262 --> 00:13:53.732
behind the eight ball
in the early stages.

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:55.634 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I talk about this
in my book a little bit,

00:13:55.634 --> 00:13:58.637
in March and April when they
weren't taking it as seriously.

00:13:58.637 --> 00:14:00.673
I mean, there were people in
the administration who were.

00:14:00.673 --> 00:14:03.108 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think the president
downplayed it a little more

00:14:03.108 --> 00:14:05.578
than he should have,
and they didn't develop

00:14:05.578 --> 00:14:07.780
a lot of national strategies.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:10.049 align:start size:84% position:16%
We had 50 individual
state strategies.

00:14:10.049 --> 00:14:13.118 align:start position:0%
I'm not saying states shouldn't
have had the authority

00:14:13.118 --> 00:14:15.254
and independence to be able
to make their own decisions,

00:14:15.254 --> 00:14:18.224
but certain things like,
we shouldn't have 50 States

00:14:18.224 --> 00:14:21.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
competing with one another
in a constrained market

00:14:21.126 --> 00:14:22.595
for desperately needed supplies,

00:14:22.595 --> 00:14:24.063
competing with the
federal government

00:14:24.063 --> 00:14:26.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
and trying to acquire
things all over the world.

00:14:26.432 --> 00:14:29.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
So on testing and
contact tracing, and some

00:14:29.802 --> 00:14:32.471
of the major issues that the
power of the federal government

00:14:32.471 --> 00:14:34.740 align:start size:88% position:13%
could really have helped
with the pandemic.

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:36.442
And in some respects
they're getting better,

00:14:36.442 --> 00:14:38.844 align:start size:84% position:16%
but certain things
the federal government

00:14:38.844 --> 00:14:40.546
can just do better.

00:14:40.546 --> 00:14:44.016
- Can you talk a little bit
about how you specifically

00:14:44.016 --> 00:14:47.887
make decisions when it comes
to lockdown or the rest of it?

00:14:47.887 --> 00:14:51.223
Because as you know, I'm here
in Los Angeles, California.

00:14:51.223 --> 00:14:55.194
We've had rolling lockdowns
then they, kicked in again.

00:14:55.194 --> 00:14:58.130
Last week I was actually out
to lunch for the first time

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:00.266
in weeks and as I
was having lunch,

00:15:00.266 --> 00:15:02.835
we got the notification
on my phone that said,

00:15:02.835 --> 00:15:05.170
the restaurants are
closing in two hours.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:06.839
Now everything's closed again.

00:15:06.839 --> 00:15:09.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I've been very
critical of Gavin Newsom,

00:15:09.141 --> 00:15:11.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
our governor here, not
necessarily because

00:15:11.744 --> 00:15:14.446 align:start size:94% position:6%
of what he's doing rather
than the messaging.

00:15:14.446 --> 00:15:17.082
It seems like they just,
they just give edicts.

00:15:17.082 --> 00:15:18.217
They never say, well,

00:15:18.217 --> 00:15:20.386 align:start size:88% position:13%
we've seen these
numbers change this way.

00:15:20.386 --> 00:15:22.621
We know this behavior
changed this or that.

00:15:22.621 --> 00:15:24.690
It's just, oh, now
we're locking down.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:27.259
We'll see you in August,
we'll see you in September.

00:15:27.259 --> 00:15:29.762
And that's what I'm sort
of more concerned about.

00:15:29.762 --> 00:15:31.597 align:start size:97% position:3%
Can you talk a little bit
about how you message things

00:15:31.597 --> 00:15:34.266
and how you actually
come to the decisions?

00:15:34.266 --> 00:15:36.001 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, it was great
question, Dave,

00:15:36.001 --> 00:15:37.803
and you hit on a couple
of really good points.

00:15:37.803 --> 00:15:41.140
First of all, I agree that the
messaging has been a problem.

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:42.908
You know, I think a
lot of the governors

00:15:42.908 --> 00:15:44.643
are not messaging very well.

00:15:44.643 --> 00:15:47.313 align:start size:97% position:3%
And frankly, I think that's
where the president's

00:15:47.313 --> 00:15:50.849 align:start size:97% position:3%
biggest weaknesses have
been, on the messaging side.

00:15:52.251 --> 00:15:55.020 align:start size:97% position:3%
I've tried to be just very
direct and upfront and provide

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:58.223
as much information as possible
because I think most people,

00:15:58.223 --> 00:16:02.328 align:start size:97% position:3%
whether the news is good or
bad, if you're just, you know,

00:16:02.328 --> 00:16:04.964 align:start size:97% position:3%
frank with them and you supply
the facts in a situation

00:16:04.964 --> 00:16:07.666
like this, when we're dealing
with, you know, in some cases,

00:16:07.666 --> 00:16:09.868
life and death matters in
a serious health crisis

00:16:09.868 --> 00:16:13.305
and a global pandemic and an
economic collapse potentially,

00:16:13.305 --> 00:16:15.207 align:start size:97% position:3%
people want to know the
facts and they want to know

00:16:15.207 --> 00:16:16.742
why you're making decisions.

00:16:16.742 --> 00:16:19.078
So, you know, I think I'm
not the only one doing it,

00:16:19.078 --> 00:16:20.279 align:start size:97% position:3%
but there are a number,
the governors who have been

00:16:20.279 --> 00:16:23.415 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
more direct and open
and transparent,

00:16:23.415 --> 00:16:25.050 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
I think have been
the most successful.

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:28.520 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
On the decision making
process, I just tried to get,

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:31.824 line:0%
you know, we had to make
decisions really quickly

00:16:31.824 --> 00:16:35.194 align:start position:0%
in this thing because it wasn't
like we have deliberations

00:16:35.194 --> 00:16:37.129
with our legislature
and let's talk about it

00:16:37.129 --> 00:16:38.630
and form a committee.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:40.899
You know, this was happening
really fast at the beginning.

00:16:40.899 --> 00:16:43.135 align:start size:97% position:3%
As we were spiking, we were
seeing things happening

00:16:43.135 --> 00:16:46.672 align:start position:0%
in Italy, in New York and people
dying and bodies stacked up

00:16:46.672 --> 00:16:49.541
in overflowing hospitals and
we were hearing these things

00:16:49.541 --> 00:16:51.110
about two million
potential deaths.

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:54.146
And so we were, time
was of the essence

00:16:54.146 --> 00:16:55.981
to make some decisions.

00:16:55.981 --> 00:16:59.218 align:start size:88% position:13%
You had to make the
right decisions quickly.

00:16:59.218 --> 00:17:02.721
You couldn't hesitate, but I
wanted to get the best advice

00:17:02.721 --> 00:17:04.456
I could from the
smartest people.

00:17:04.456 --> 00:17:07.760 align:start size:97% position:3%
We put together a taskforce
of really smart doctors

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:09.828
and scientists and
business people.

00:17:09.828 --> 00:17:11.930
And I just tried to
get the best input,

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:14.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
listen to everyone and
then make decisions

00:17:14.967 --> 00:17:17.870
without hesitating and then
communicate it really well

00:17:17.870 --> 00:17:19.905 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the people about,
this is what we're doing

00:17:19.905 --> 00:17:22.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
and here's why, and here's
how it's going to help.

00:17:22.541 --> 00:17:25.110 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so we took very
aggressive and early actions,

00:17:25.110 --> 00:17:28.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
did things that I never would
have imagined ever doing,

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:29.915
but it helped us
flatten the curve.

00:17:29.915 --> 00:17:31.316
I mean, our numbers
are really good.

00:17:31.316 --> 00:17:33.152
Our economy's really good.

00:17:33.152 --> 00:17:34.753
We took early and
aggressive action.

00:17:34.753 --> 00:17:38.057
We had a safe and effective
reopening and, you know,

00:17:38.057 --> 00:17:40.325
we're now, you know,
way down from our peak

00:17:40.325 --> 00:17:42.628 align:start size:84% position:16%
that happened about
more than 90 days ago.

00:17:42.628 --> 00:17:44.563 align:start size:84% position:16%
We've opened up
98% of our economy.

00:17:44.563 --> 00:17:47.232
And our unemployment
at 8% is staggering,

00:17:47.232 --> 00:17:50.636
but it's, you know, some are
two and a half times worse,

00:17:50.636 --> 00:17:54.206 align:start size:97% position:3%
better than the rest of
the country and our numbers

00:17:54.206 --> 00:17:57.009
while, you know, we we've
had a number of deaths

00:17:57.009 --> 00:17:59.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we had the same
problems other people had,

00:17:59.611 --> 00:18:01.380
we're in great shape right now.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:03.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, on a personal
note as a guy

00:18:03.382 --> 00:18:05.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
that didn't want to
always get into politics,

00:18:05.451 --> 00:18:07.252
when you have to make
those decisions about,

00:18:07.252 --> 00:18:08.854
okay, we're going to
extend the lockdown,

00:18:08.854 --> 00:18:11.990
we're gonna lift the lock
down, whatever it may be,

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:13.492
what was that like for you?

00:18:13.492 --> 00:18:15.928 align:start size:97% position:3%
'Cause I think people think
of people think of politicians

00:18:15.928 --> 00:18:18.063
sort of as robots, like, oh,
they just make the choices.

00:18:18.063 --> 00:18:19.298 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- But, yeah.

00:18:19.298 --> 00:18:22.367
- No, it was really,
really hard, Dave.

00:18:22.367 --> 00:18:24.937 align:start position:0%
You know, I've been through
and I talk about some of these,

00:18:24.937 --> 00:18:27.739
you know, personal and
professional struggles,

00:18:27.739 --> 00:18:29.708 align:start size:84% position:16%
battles that I've been
through in my book.

00:18:29.708 --> 00:18:31.877
And I share some of that,
you know, what it was like,

00:18:31.877 --> 00:18:33.712 align:start size:88% position:13%
and all the things
that I had to deal with

00:18:33.712 --> 00:18:36.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
on a personal level or
what battles I went through

00:18:36.448 --> 00:18:38.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
as governor or getting
elected governor.

00:18:38.984 --> 00:18:41.353 align:start size:94% position:6%
This was the hardest thing
that I think I've had

00:18:41.353 --> 00:18:45.557
to deal with, something we
never imagined dealing with.

00:18:45.557 --> 00:18:47.059
And it wasn't just, you know,

00:18:47.059 --> 00:18:48.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
I went through a cancer
battle where I was worried

00:18:48.894 --> 00:18:51.630 align:start size:97% position:3%
about how my family was going
to take it, or, you know,

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:54.900 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was dealing with
other cancer patients.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.202
This was, I was worried
about six million people

00:18:57.202 --> 00:18:59.638 align:start size:97% position:3%
in my state, and my decisions
were going to impact

00:18:59.638 --> 00:19:01.773 align:start size:84% position:16%
not only their health,
but I'm a lifelong

00:19:01.773 --> 00:19:04.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
small business guy, ran for
governor with the sole purpose

00:19:04.676 --> 00:19:06.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
of creating jobs and
helping small businesses,

00:19:06.745 --> 00:19:08.247
and decisions I
was going to make

00:19:08.247 --> 00:19:10.482 align:start size:97% position:3%
were going to impact, it was
going to cost people jobs

00:19:10.482 --> 00:19:12.151
and hurt their small businesses,

00:19:12.151 --> 00:19:13.619
or people were going to die.

00:19:14.753 --> 00:19:17.856
It was a really hard
situation to be put in.

00:19:17.856 --> 00:19:19.958 align:start size:97% position:3%
And, frankly, the president,
et cetera, left it up.

00:19:19.958 --> 00:19:21.627
He said, it's up
to the governors.

00:19:21.627 --> 00:19:25.998
And we were getting, you
know, open up and then,

00:19:25.998 --> 00:19:27.699
you know, but you
have to shut down.

00:19:27.699 --> 00:19:29.935
He was giving, the
administration was
given us advice.

00:19:29.935 --> 00:19:31.470
You have to do these things,

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:32.871
or we're gonna have
two million deaths.

00:19:32.871 --> 00:19:35.707 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then, you know,
messaging the opposite stuff

00:19:35.707 --> 00:19:38.644
the next day so it was, we
were in tough situations,

00:19:38.644 --> 00:19:40.579
but I think, and everybody
made different decisions,

00:19:40.579 --> 00:19:43.482
but on a personal level, I mean,

00:19:43.482 --> 00:19:46.218 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's been very little
sleep for five months

00:19:46.218 --> 00:19:47.753
and a lot of stress.

00:19:47.753 --> 00:19:49.788
And you've got people that
are mad at you either way,

00:19:49.788 --> 00:19:53.091
no matter what you do, you
can't make everybody happy.

00:19:53.091 --> 00:19:55.894
And, you know, I just tried
to do the very best I could,

00:19:55.894 --> 00:19:58.263 align:start size:88% position:13%
based on the information
I had and, you know,

00:19:58.263 --> 00:20:01.567
tried to do the best thing
for the people in the state.

00:20:01.567 --> 00:20:04.970
- Yeah, so let's talk about the
other thing that's going on.

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:06.672 align:start size:88% position:13%
'Cause there is
another thing going on.

00:20:06.672 --> 00:20:08.207
There's always a lot of things,

00:20:08.207 --> 00:20:09.675
but there's sort of two main
ones going on at the moment.

00:20:09.675 --> 00:20:13.679 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the second one obviously
is, I guess the best way

00:20:13.679 --> 00:20:16.181 align:start size:88% position:13%
you describe it is
either the racial unrest

00:20:16.181 --> 00:20:18.483
or the protesting
or the rioting,

00:20:18.483 --> 00:20:20.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
just the whole
conversation we're having.

00:20:20.285 --> 00:20:22.821
The toppling of, of monuments.

00:20:22.821 --> 00:20:25.524 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now, again, you're in
Maryland, D.C. is right there.

00:20:25.524 --> 00:20:28.427 align:start size:94% position:6%
An awful lot of monuments,
an awful lot of history

00:20:28.427 --> 00:20:30.362
is right in your backyard.

00:20:31.496 --> 00:20:33.365 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, you've
got Baltimore there,

00:20:33.365 --> 00:20:36.568
which has its own set
of set of problems.

00:20:36.568 --> 00:20:38.070
So I'm not even going
to ask you a question.

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:39.638
I'm just going to just
pass all that off to you.

00:20:39.638 --> 00:20:41.440 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Toss it out.
- Take it away, yeah.

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:45.244
- Yeah, well, that's, you're
just gonna let me tee it up.

00:20:47.079 --> 00:20:48.814
You know, you look at
the title of the book.

00:20:48.814 --> 00:20:52.451
It's like riots, you
know, cancer, riots,
a global pandemic.

00:20:52.451 --> 00:20:55.187
I dealt with this and I
talk about this a lot.

00:20:55.187 --> 00:20:57.623
And there's real parallels
to what's going on right now

00:20:57.623 --> 00:21:01.660 align:start size:97% position:3%
with the riots, the violence,
the unrest, the protests,

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:04.062
however you want to describe it.

00:21:04.062 --> 00:21:05.631
There's a little bit
of all of that going on

00:21:05.631 --> 00:21:07.399
across America in
different cities.

00:21:08.567 --> 00:21:12.804
In 2015, right after I
became governor, we had,

00:21:12.804 --> 00:21:14.806 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'd only been
governor for 89 days.

00:21:14.806 --> 00:21:17.609 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we had the worst violence
in 47 years break out

00:21:17.609 --> 00:21:19.111
in our largest city in Baltimore

00:21:19.111 --> 00:21:21.213
after the death of Freddie Gray.

00:21:21.213 --> 00:21:24.516
And, you know, in the
very first few hours

00:21:24.516 --> 00:21:27.986
in Baltimore City,
400 businesses were
burned and looted

00:21:27.986 --> 00:21:31.123 align:start size:88% position:13%
and destroyed, 127
police and firefighters

00:21:31.123 --> 00:21:32.991
were injured and hospitalized.

00:21:32.991 --> 00:21:37.329
The city police force was
overwhelmed and the city

00:21:37.329 --> 00:21:40.232
was really having no
ability to respond.

00:21:40.232 --> 00:21:42.267
It was sort of out of control.

00:21:42.267 --> 00:21:47.239 align:start size:97% position:3%
And as governor, I had
declared a state of emergency,

00:21:48.473 --> 00:21:49.641
sent in 4,000 members
of the National Guard

00:21:49.641 --> 00:21:51.777 align:start size:88% position:13%
and 1,000 police
officers into the city.

00:21:53.178 --> 00:21:56.214 align:start size:97% position:3%
We allowed peaceful protests
to go on for a week,

00:21:56.214 --> 00:21:57.683
but we shut down the violence.

00:21:57.683 --> 00:22:00.152 align:start size:84% position:16%
We stopped the
burning, the looting

00:22:00.152 --> 00:22:02.821
and all the damage
that was being done.

00:22:02.821 --> 00:22:04.990
And, you know, I
write about this,

00:22:04.990 --> 00:22:08.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think I have five chapters
on this in the book.

00:22:08.093 --> 00:22:10.662 align:start size:94% position:6%
I walked the streets of
Baltimore for a solid week

00:22:10.662 --> 00:22:12.698
to listen to people,
that was hugging people

00:22:12.698 --> 00:22:14.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
who lost their businesses
and their homes.

00:22:14.599 --> 00:22:16.501
I was meeting with
community leaders.

00:22:16.501 --> 00:22:18.670 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was talking to
faith-based leaders.

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:21.940
I was walking the streets where
Freddie Gray's neighborhood.

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:24.843
I was, you know, I was
dealing with the crisis,

00:22:24.843 --> 00:22:27.546 align:start size:84% position:16%
but also trying to
lower the temperature.

00:22:27.546 --> 00:22:31.550 align:start position:0%
And I think we found the
balance of, I call it, you know,

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:33.018
Reagan's peace through strength.

00:22:33.018 --> 00:22:36.321 align:start size:97% position:3%
We sent enough people in there
to keep the citizens safe,

00:22:36.321 --> 00:22:39.424 align:start size:88% position:13%
but not allow the
violence and destruction

00:22:39.424 --> 00:22:42.094 align:start size:84% position:16%
without further
inflaming, you know,

00:22:42.094 --> 00:22:46.198
the anger and violence by
moving too aggressively.

00:22:46.198 --> 00:22:47.733
You know, we just, you know,

00:22:47.733 --> 00:22:50.168
if you were protesting in
the streets, it was OK.

00:22:50.168 --> 00:22:53.138
If you're throwing rocks
and setting things on fire,

00:22:53.138 --> 00:22:54.506
we're going to arrest you.

00:22:54.506 --> 00:22:56.007 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think the
mistakes that were made

00:22:56.007 --> 00:22:58.777 align:start size:97% position:3%
in some of the large cities
today, by just letting,

00:22:58.777 --> 00:23:01.279
you know, mob rule takeover,

00:23:01.279 --> 00:23:05.150
and just not to respond
at all was a big mistake.

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:08.687 align:start size:88% position:13%
There's obviously
legitimate frustrations

00:23:08.687 --> 00:23:12.090
in the black community
and I was very proud

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:14.092
of actually Baltimore this time,

00:23:14.092 --> 00:23:16.094 align:start size:84% position:16%
as we're seeing things
erupt everywhere,

00:23:16.094 --> 00:23:20.265
it was mostly very peaceful
protests without the violence

00:23:20.265 --> 00:23:21.800
and destruction this time.

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:26.071 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think our police,
state police, National Guard,

00:23:26.071 --> 00:23:28.140 align:start position:0%
and the city police in
Baltimore did a really good job.

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:30.108 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think the citizens
here did a good job.

00:23:30.108 --> 00:23:32.878
They were actually pointing
out the handful of, you know,

00:23:32.878 --> 00:23:34.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
violent folks that
weren't even from the city

00:23:34.746 --> 00:23:37.516
and saying, hey, stop this.

00:23:37.516 --> 00:23:40.685 align:start size:97% position:3%
They wanted to just peacefully
express their frustrations.

00:23:40.685 --> 00:23:43.488
But it's something we've
got to get a handle on.

00:23:43.488 --> 00:23:46.391 line:0%
And I, you know, I understand
both sides of the argument,

00:23:46.391 --> 00:23:48.193 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
but I talk about this
extensively in the book.

00:23:48.193 --> 00:23:50.395 line:0%
I think you've got to, you know,

00:23:50.395 --> 00:23:52.631 line:0%
we've got to find the way
to get this under control

00:23:52.631 --> 00:23:55.267 align:start size:84% position:16%
while addressing
some of the concerns.

00:23:55.267 --> 00:23:58.970
- How miserable of a
decision is it for you

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:01.373 align:start size:97% position:3%
when something's going
down in one of your cities,

00:24:01.373 --> 00:24:04.142
in this case, Baltimore,
where you have to then

00:24:04.142 --> 00:24:07.512 align:start size:97% position:3%
bring in the National Guard
or something else where I know

00:24:07.512 --> 00:24:10.482
it's your preference to let
the mayor hopefully do his

00:24:10.482 --> 00:24:13.251
or her job and make sure that
they take care of business.

00:24:13.251 --> 00:24:15.387
But as we're seeing in a lot
of these states right now,

00:24:15.387 --> 00:24:17.622
I mean, we're really seeing
it in Portland right now.

00:24:17.622 --> 00:24:21.560 align:start position:0%
The mayor seems either unwilling
or unable to do anything.

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:23.929
And now we've got,
we've got in effect,

00:24:23.929 --> 00:24:26.598
we've got federal troops
in the streets of Portland.

00:24:26.598 --> 00:24:30.569 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, they're making,
the mayor, I think

00:24:30.569 --> 00:24:34.005
by not taking action early,
I think they made a mistake.

00:24:34.005 --> 00:24:38.310 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I was very
prepared for that.

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:41.112
I kind of saw what
might be happening.

00:24:41.112 --> 00:24:42.914
Our team, we stood up

00:24:42.914 --> 00:24:44.816 align:start size:84% position:16%
the Maryland Emergency
Management Agency

00:24:44.816 --> 00:24:46.251
in about a week in advance.

00:24:46.251 --> 00:24:49.120 align:start position:0%
We saw things potentially
bubble up a couple days earlier.

00:24:49.120 --> 00:24:51.256
I was ready with
executive orders,

00:24:51.256 --> 00:24:52.524
you know, already prepared.

00:24:52.524 --> 00:24:54.659
I've been in constant
contact with the city.

00:24:54.659 --> 00:24:57.062
The first violence that
violence had broke out

00:24:57.062 --> 00:24:59.931 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was on the phone with
the mayor saying, you know,

00:24:59.931 --> 00:25:02.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
this doesn't look like
you have it under control.

00:25:02.267 --> 00:25:05.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
I took action within a
matter of hours to come in

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:08.640 align:start size:81% position:19%
and back up the
beleaguered police

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:10.275 align:start size:84% position:16%
that were overwhelmed
in the city.

00:25:10.275 --> 00:25:13.078
And I think, you know, that
early and aggressive action,

00:25:13.078 --> 00:25:14.880
again, it's sort of
like this pandemic.

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:19.150
There's no time to delay and
hesitate and to be too passive.

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:22.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
But again, we didn't go
in there and aggressively,

00:25:22.587 --> 00:25:27.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, cause further
escalation of the problem.

00:25:27.092 --> 00:25:28.927
We just went in there to
keep the citizens safe.

00:25:28.927 --> 00:25:30.395
And they were very
appreciative of it.

00:25:30.395 --> 00:25:33.331
You know, the people
in Baltimore were like,

00:25:33.331 --> 00:25:34.866
thank you, because
they were scared.

00:25:34.866 --> 00:25:36.268 align:start size:88% position:13%
They were really worried
about this violence

00:25:36.268 --> 00:25:38.970 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the people trying
to take over their city.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:41.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Are you hearing from
just regular people

00:25:41.273 --> 00:25:44.409
that they're really worried
about the sort of mob rule

00:25:44.409 --> 00:25:46.111
that seems to be taking effect,

00:25:46.111 --> 00:25:48.580 align:start size:94% position:6%
regardless of whether
you're for statues stand,

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:52.217 align:start position:0%
the fact that they are just
coming down all over the place,

00:25:52.217 --> 00:25:56.087
as opposed to let's,
you know, let's vote

00:25:56.087 --> 00:25:57.856
and make a decision
or have, you know,

00:25:57.856 --> 00:26:00.258
some process related to the law.

00:26:00.258 --> 00:26:01.826 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, whether you
want them down or not,

00:26:01.826 --> 00:26:04.663
I think most people agree
that tearing them down

00:26:04.663 --> 00:26:06.097 align:start size:72% position:28%
is not legal.
- Right.

00:26:06.097 --> 00:26:08.934 align:start size:97% position:3%
At the very least, are regular
people bringing that up

00:26:08.934 --> 00:26:10.335
to you, that the
rule of law seems

00:26:10.335 --> 00:26:12.771 align:start size:81% position:19%
to be up in the
air at the moment?

00:26:12.771 --> 00:26:17.676
- Well to me, I'm shocked that
this is even a debate really.

00:26:17.676 --> 00:26:19.711
I mean, we had, we did have,

00:26:19.711 --> 00:26:22.347 align:start size:88% position:13%
although I was praising
Baltimore this time

00:26:22.347 --> 00:26:25.250
about the lack of violence,
we did have a situation where

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:29.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
in Little Italy,
we had somebody, a
smaller group of folks

00:26:29.521 --> 00:26:32.123 align:start size:88% position:13%
tore down a Christopher
Columbus statue.

00:26:32.123 --> 00:26:34.859
Now I'm not here to
debate the merits

00:26:34.859 --> 00:26:37.128 align:start position:0%
of whether Christopher Columbus
was a great guy or not,

00:26:37.128 --> 00:26:40.432
but there's no way that we
should encourage, you know,

00:26:40.432 --> 00:26:41.800
mobs to be able to tear down

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:44.069
whatever piece of art
or history they want to

00:26:44.069 --> 00:26:46.404
instead of going before the
city council and arguing

00:26:46.404 --> 00:26:48.306
why they think they
maybe should put that

00:26:48.306 --> 00:26:50.208
in a museum or do
something else.

00:26:50.208 --> 00:26:52.944 align:start size:97% position:3%
We actually had a city
councilman member in Baltimore

00:26:52.944 --> 00:26:55.480 align:start size:88% position:13%
while they were tearing
down that statue

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:57.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
that tweeted something
like, you know,

00:26:57.048 --> 00:26:59.951
maybe they should consider
going down and tearing down

00:26:59.951 --> 00:27:03.355
another statue that
was built to honor

00:27:03.355 --> 00:27:06.858
fallen police officers, a
memorial that was put up

00:27:06.858 --> 00:27:09.794
by the families of, I mean,
it was just unbelievable

00:27:09.794 --> 00:27:11.796
that we're having these
kinds of discussions.

00:27:11.796 --> 00:27:15.066 align:start size:97% position:3%
I understand the debate
about some of these statutes.

00:27:15.066 --> 00:27:17.168
Maybe we really should
take a look at, you know,

00:27:17.168 --> 00:27:19.004
making a decision
to remove them.

00:27:19.004 --> 00:27:20.305
And I think that's a debate.

00:27:20.305 --> 00:27:21.439
That's an argument
and a discussion

00:27:21.439 --> 00:27:23.341
we definitely should have.

00:27:23.341 --> 00:27:25.143
And some of them,
I understand why

00:27:25.143 --> 00:27:27.812
they may be offensive to some
folk, but you can't just allow

00:27:27.812 --> 00:27:30.849
mobs to decide which ones
they want to tear down.

00:27:30.849 --> 00:27:34.352
- Yeah, well, we seem to be
in this sort of mob moment,

00:27:34.352 --> 00:27:36.388
I suppose you could call it.

00:27:37.756 --> 00:27:40.792 align:start size:97% position:3%
What are you seeing between
the tension right now

00:27:40.792 --> 00:27:42.761 align:start size:88% position:13%
between the Republicans
and the Democrats?

00:27:42.761 --> 00:27:44.496
You strike me as,
what I would say

00:27:44.496 --> 00:27:46.564
is a pretty moderate Republican.

00:27:46.564 --> 00:27:50.435
You can either, you can
assess that statement

00:27:50.435 --> 00:27:52.704 align:start size:97% position:3%
however you'd like, but
that's what you strike me as.

00:27:52.704 --> 00:27:55.240 align:start size:84% position:16%
What I'm worried about
is that, you know,

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:56.675
I don't know how much
you know about me,

00:27:56.675 --> 00:28:00.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I was a lifelong
Democrat lefty progressive

00:28:00.178 --> 00:28:01.446
until the last couple of years.

00:28:01.446 --> 00:28:03.548 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I know.
- And I'm worried about--

00:28:03.548 --> 00:28:07.152
- And then you saw the light.
- (laughs) And here we are,

00:28:07.152 --> 00:28:09.688 align:start size:97% position:3%
right, and here we are,
but what I am worried about

00:28:09.688 --> 00:28:11.923
is that my former
side, so to speak,

00:28:11.923 --> 00:28:14.459
and I don't like doing it
in sides but, you know,

00:28:14.459 --> 00:28:16.761
there's only so many ways we
can talk about these things,

00:28:16.761 --> 00:28:18.430
that my former side,
that it seems like

00:28:18.430 --> 00:28:21.199 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you don't go fully
woke, if you don't go fully

00:28:21.199 --> 00:28:26.204 align:start size:84% position:16%
to where the Bernie
AOC energy is

00:28:27.338 --> 00:28:28.573 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they're just
going to take you out.

00:28:28.573 --> 00:28:31.810
And are you seeing that
at a state level, too?

00:28:31.810 --> 00:28:33.578
- Oh yeah, no, there's
no question about that.

00:28:33.578 --> 00:28:37.615
And so, yeah, look, I'm
a lifelong Republican.

00:28:37.615 --> 00:28:40.385
I was a chairman of Youth for
Reagan and I was in, you know,

00:28:40.385 --> 00:28:42.454
I would say most of my life,

00:28:42.454 --> 00:28:44.823 align:start size:97% position:3%
I would be considered a
conservative, a Republican.

00:28:44.823 --> 00:28:47.659
Now I guess they call me a
moderate because, you know,

00:28:47.659 --> 00:28:51.062
I really try to avoid the
extremes of either party.

00:28:51.062 --> 00:28:53.164 align:start position:0%
And I think one of the
problems we have today is that,

00:28:53.164 --> 00:28:55.567
you know, first of all,

00:28:55.567 --> 00:28:58.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
I like to avoid the
divisive, angry rhetoric.

00:28:58.336 --> 00:29:00.371
And because I want to work
together to get things done,

00:29:00.371 --> 00:29:02.006 align:start size:81% position:19%
that makes me a
moderate, I guess.

00:29:02.006 --> 00:29:05.243
I don't think, you know,
trying to get things done

00:29:05.243 --> 00:29:08.680 align:start size:94% position:6%
and finding compromise,
you know, is a dirty word.

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:10.381
Compromise isn't a bad thing.

00:29:10.381 --> 00:29:12.951 align:start size:97% position:3%
Doing nothing is a bad
thing, getting nothing done,

00:29:12.951 --> 00:29:15.353
standing up, I'm going to
stick to my principles,

00:29:15.353 --> 00:29:17.021 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I'm not gonna
accomplish anything.

00:29:17.021 --> 00:29:19.824
But, you know, right now I
think the Democratic Party

00:29:19.824 --> 00:29:21.326
is moving too far to the left.

00:29:21.326 --> 00:29:25.163 align:start position:0%
And I think the Republican Party
is now shrinking their base

00:29:25.163 --> 00:29:27.065
down to not appeal
to enough people.

00:29:27.065 --> 00:29:29.701
And there's a growing
a number of people

00:29:29.701 --> 00:29:32.904 align:start position:0%
that are either moderate or
right of center, left of center,

00:29:32.904 --> 00:29:34.272
who are somewhat frustrated

00:29:34.272 --> 00:29:36.574 align:start size:81% position:19%
with the entire
political process.

00:29:36.574 --> 00:29:38.877
And they're like, this
is the best we can do?

00:29:38.877 --> 00:29:42.247
And you know, they're not really

00:29:42.247 --> 00:29:44.783
on the extremes of
either party in mind.

00:29:44.783 --> 00:29:46.184 align:start size:84% position:16%
You see polling
that says, you know,

00:29:46.184 --> 00:29:49.287 align:start size:97% position:3%
maybe 15% consider
themselves very progressive

00:29:49.287 --> 00:29:51.890 align:start size:84% position:16%
or very conservative
on each side.

00:29:51.890 --> 00:29:54.392 align:start size:88% position:13%
And everybody else is
somewhere in the middle.

00:29:54.392 --> 00:29:56.361
And they're saying--
- How do we fix that?

00:29:56.361 --> 00:29:58.296
- Exhausted majority, that goes,

00:29:58.296 --> 00:30:00.431
why can't we get
somebody normal?

00:30:00.431 --> 00:30:02.267
And why can't we
get something done?

00:30:02.267 --> 00:30:04.869 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So how do you fix
it, how do you fix it?

00:30:04.869 --> 00:30:08.039
It's hard to fix it, but I
mean, part of it is, I mean,

00:30:08.039 --> 00:30:10.241
it's a broke, look some
people have given up

00:30:10.241 --> 00:30:12.710
and they're like, we're never
gonna fix the broken politics.

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:15.280 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, I would argue that
some of that we've been,

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:19.851
and I talk about this in
my book, I think you can,

00:30:19.851 --> 00:30:22.020
you know, lower the temperature,

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:24.222
try to avoid the
divisive rhetoric,

00:30:24.222 --> 00:30:27.959 align:start size:97% position:3%
try to find ways to actually
work in a pragmatic way.

00:30:27.959 --> 00:30:30.328
But there's also structural
things that we have to do.

00:30:30.328 --> 00:30:32.230
I mean, we got to get
rid of gerrymandering

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:35.233 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the way our primary
process works only

00:30:35.233 --> 00:30:37.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
the people on the
extremes win the primary.

00:30:37.869 --> 00:30:39.771 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's no competitive
races anywhere

00:30:39.771 --> 00:30:41.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the general
elections typically.

00:30:41.472 --> 00:30:42.941
And we end up with, you know,

00:30:42.941 --> 00:30:45.210
people in Congress
that are, you know,

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:47.078
not ever going to
agree on anything

00:30:47.078 --> 00:30:49.547
and not ever meet in
the middle on anything.

00:30:49.547 --> 00:30:54.319
So I think we've got to kind
of work on, how do we elect,

00:30:54.319 --> 00:30:56.187
you know, people that
are willing to go down

00:30:56.187 --> 00:30:57.755
and work together
and get, I mean,

00:30:57.755 --> 00:31:00.258
I think people are frustrated
whether they are on the right

00:31:00.258 --> 00:31:01.759
or the left about the fact

00:31:01.759 --> 00:31:03.728 align:start size:81% position:19%
that nobody can
get anything done.

00:31:03.728 --> 00:31:04.963
- Yeah.

00:31:04.963 --> 00:31:06.464
- I'm willing to
work with anybody.

00:31:06.464 --> 00:31:07.699
I'm kind of on the, get to
work and get things done

00:31:07.699 --> 00:31:08.967
school of politics.

00:31:08.967 --> 00:31:10.468
I don't care if you're a
Democrat or Republican,

00:31:10.468 --> 00:31:12.270 line:0%
if you'll help me
fix the problem.

00:31:13.438 --> 00:31:14.906 line:0%
- Yeah, and that's sort
of been your position

00:31:14.906 --> 00:31:16.307 line:0%
with Trump, right?

00:31:16.307 --> 00:31:17.542 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
Because you've obviously,
I mean, even here

00:31:17.542 --> 00:31:19.644 line:0%
you've been critical of
some of his responses,

00:31:19.644 --> 00:31:20.879 line:0%
but then there's
some other things

00:31:20.879 --> 00:31:23.014
that you think he's
done pretty well.

00:31:23.014 --> 00:31:24.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, no, well,
look, I've never been,

00:31:24.716 --> 00:31:27.118 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm one of those people
you've never seen me as one

00:31:27.118 --> 00:31:30.054 align:start position:0%
of the guys that's on constantly
criticizing the president

00:31:30.054 --> 00:31:31.623
for no reason, never done that.

00:31:31.623 --> 00:31:34.259
Never been going after
him in a personal way.

00:31:34.259 --> 00:31:37.896 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's plenty of people on
TV that'll do that constantly,

00:31:37.896 --> 00:31:39.831
but I'm also never, I'm not
one of those Republicans

00:31:39.831 --> 00:31:42.467 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's afraid to speak
up if I disagree,

00:31:42.467 --> 00:31:44.168
which, you know,
a lot of them are.

00:31:44.168 --> 00:31:46.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just pretty much, like,
I tell it like it is.

00:31:46.304 --> 00:31:49.641 align:start position:0%
I mean, I, you know, I
respect, I praise the president

00:31:49.641 --> 00:31:51.676
when they do something right.

00:31:51.676 --> 00:31:54.078
And, I agree with him
when I think, you know,

00:31:54.078 --> 00:31:56.447 align:start size:97% position:3%
he's in the right on
something, but I always say,

00:31:56.447 --> 00:31:58.349 align:start size:84% position:16%
if they haven't
done something well,

00:31:58.349 --> 00:32:00.518
I'm not going to just
sit down and shut up

00:32:00.518 --> 00:32:02.520
and then keep it to myself.

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:05.456 align:start size:97% position:3%
And there are a lot of
Republicans who agree with me,

00:32:05.456 --> 00:32:07.292
but they're afraid to say it.

00:32:07.292 --> 00:32:08.927
- Does it ever sort of kill you

00:32:08.927 --> 00:32:11.429
that like a nice guy like you,

00:32:11.429 --> 00:32:13.998
that Trump was willing
to do all the things

00:32:13.998 --> 00:32:16.000
that maybe somebody like
you wouldn't have done?

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:17.268
And when I say someone like you,

00:32:17.268 --> 00:32:18.703
I mean, someone like me, too,

00:32:18.703 --> 00:32:21.940 align:start position:0%
that there is sort of more
moderate people that maybe just

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:26.911
won't do the things that it
takes to win at that level.

00:32:26.911 --> 00:32:28.179
It's not even a judgment.

00:32:28.179 --> 00:32:30.548
It just sort of is that
we're all sort of wired

00:32:30.548 --> 00:32:32.583
a little differently, we
all take the temperature

00:32:32.583 --> 00:32:33.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the room a little
bit differently.

00:32:33.851 --> 00:32:38.189
- Yeah, you know, look,
I'm willing to stand up

00:32:38.189 --> 00:32:40.291 align:start size:88% position:13%
and fight for the things
that I believe in,

00:32:40.291 --> 00:32:43.628 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I'm not for divisiveness
and dysfunction.

00:32:43.628 --> 00:32:47.332 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I would argue
that people liked me.

00:32:47.332 --> 00:32:50.134
And I look at our record
over the past six years,

00:32:50.134 --> 00:32:52.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
we've been more effective
and can get more things done.

00:32:52.837 --> 00:32:56.708
I'm not sure, and it's not
talking about philosophy now,

00:32:56.708 --> 00:32:58.443
or we're not agreeing with many

00:32:58.443 --> 00:33:01.112
of the president's
positions, philosophy

00:33:01.112 --> 00:33:02.914 align:start size:81% position:19%
I would agree with
on the economy.

00:33:02.914 --> 00:33:05.516
And I think he's done some
things that are pretty good,

00:33:05.516 --> 00:33:07.285
but he hasn't gotten a
lot done in Congress.

00:33:07.285 --> 00:33:10.521
And some of it is
that personality,

00:33:10.521 --> 00:33:14.559
and some of it is, I think
he could be more successful.

00:33:14.559 --> 00:33:16.427 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think sometimes he's
his own worst enemy

00:33:16.427 --> 00:33:20.365
and he could be more effective
if he tried to do some things

00:33:20.365 --> 00:33:22.166 align:start size:84% position:16%
a little differently.
- Yeah.

00:33:22.166 --> 00:33:24.936
- So, yeah, I get it, you
gotta stand up and fight,

00:33:24.936 --> 00:33:27.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
but sometimes that's
not the skillset

00:33:27.205 --> 00:33:29.974
that you need to use
to get things done.

00:33:29.974 --> 00:33:32.810 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Hmm, let's talk
about the personal side

00:33:32.810 --> 00:33:34.212
of the book a little bit.

00:33:34.212 --> 00:33:38.483 align:start position:0%
You talk about battling
cancer, so let's talk about it.

00:33:38.483 --> 00:33:42.086
- Yeah, so I talked
about the riots.

00:33:42.086 --> 00:33:45.089 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, that you know,
right after I became governor.

00:33:45.089 --> 00:33:46.524
And I went through that.

00:33:46.524 --> 00:33:48.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'd been through my first
legislative session,

00:33:48.393 --> 00:33:50.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is a 90-day session
here in Annapolis.

00:33:50.728 --> 00:33:54.766
And I was on my first
trade mission to Asia.

00:33:54.766 --> 00:33:56.567
I went to Korea,
China, and Japan.

00:33:56.567 --> 00:33:59.337
And I noticed a
lump in my throat

00:33:59.337 --> 00:34:00.972 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I was feeling
some aches and pains.

00:34:00.972 --> 00:34:02.106
And I decided that, you know,

00:34:02.106 --> 00:34:04.375
go to a doctor to check it out.

00:34:04.375 --> 00:34:06.477
I just wasn't feeling terrible.

00:34:06.477 --> 00:34:09.013 align:start size:88% position:13%
I just felt a little
rundown and a few things

00:34:09.013 --> 00:34:12.216
that I'd noticed, and
I had three doctors,

00:34:12.216 --> 00:34:14.786 align:start size:88% position:13%
come in and tell me
that I had very advanced

00:34:14.786 --> 00:34:16.754
and aggressive cancer.

00:34:16.754 --> 00:34:20.258 align:start size:88% position:13%
From my neck to my groin
I had 50-some tumors,

00:34:21.626 --> 00:34:24.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it was a rapidly
growing, life-threatening,

00:34:25.530 --> 00:34:27.765 align:start size:88% position:13%
large B cell
non-Hodgkin's lymphoma.

00:34:27.765 --> 00:34:30.001
And it was pretty grim news.

00:34:30.001 --> 00:34:33.137
They told me, I'd been
governor for five months.

00:34:33.137 --> 00:34:37.108
It was Friday of Father's Day
weekend when I got this news.

00:34:37.108 --> 00:34:39.610
And I, you know,
I had to go home

00:34:39.610 --> 00:34:41.846
and it hit me out of the blue.

00:34:41.846 --> 00:34:44.449
I had no idea, I mean, you
know, that this was going on.

00:34:44.449 --> 00:34:46.250
I don't know how these things
were growing inside of me.

00:34:46.250 --> 00:34:48.119 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I didn't know, I'd
been through all that work,

00:34:48.119 --> 00:34:50.621
winning the campaign, going
through all that, you know,

00:34:50.621 --> 00:34:53.758
really working hard, you
know, 10, 12 hour days,

00:34:53.758 --> 00:34:56.060
seven days a week,
and still working

00:34:56.060 --> 00:34:57.862
like the Energizer bunny.

00:34:57.862 --> 00:35:00.264 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I, you know, the
hardest part was,

00:35:00.264 --> 00:35:01.732 align:start size:84% position:16%
how am I gonna explain
this to my family?

00:35:01.732 --> 00:35:05.503 align:start size:97% position:3%
I went home and told my
wife and my three daughters

00:35:05.503 --> 00:35:07.138
who were there for
Father's Day weekend.

00:35:07.138 --> 00:35:10.608
And then I had to explain
it to my 80-year-old dad

00:35:10.608 --> 00:35:12.610
who was over for Father's Day.

00:35:12.610 --> 00:35:16.180
And then I went and had
to explain it to my staff

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:18.449
who had worked so
hard and, just,

00:35:18.449 --> 00:35:21.252 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, helping us put
the administration together.

00:35:21.252 --> 00:35:22.887 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then I had to tell the
six million people in Maryland

00:35:22.887 --> 00:35:25.123
who just put their faith in me.

00:35:25.123 --> 00:35:27.692
And it was a, it was
a difficult thing.

00:35:27.692 --> 00:35:30.461
I was going through 24
hour a day chemotherapy.

00:35:30.461 --> 00:35:32.463
I went through about
an 18-month battle.

00:35:33.598 --> 00:35:35.233
I was very open and transparent.

00:35:35.233 --> 00:35:38.002
And I mean, I shared
I was going through.

00:35:38.002 --> 00:35:42.640 align:start position:0%
And it was, you know, it an
incredible, you know, I really,

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:45.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think I have a different
perspective on life

00:35:45.643 --> 00:35:48.412
and it made me realize
the important things.

00:35:48.412 --> 00:35:51.149 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I got to meet
some incredible people

00:35:51.149 --> 00:35:53.251
who were going through much
tougher battles than mine

00:35:53.251 --> 00:35:54.986
and got to meet their families.

00:35:54.986 --> 00:35:57.021 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's a whole
nother focus for me.

00:35:57.021 --> 00:36:00.525 align:start size:97% position:3%
And even if I'm not a
governor, it gives me, I mean,

00:36:00.525 --> 00:36:03.060
I'm going to continue to
fight, to help, you know,

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:06.464
raise awareness and raise money
for these terrible diseases.

00:36:07.665 --> 00:36:09.400
I mean, so many people
are touched by cancer.

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:11.702 align:start size:88% position:13%
Everywhere I go, because
of my experience,

00:36:11.702 --> 00:36:13.671
people know about it,
and they'll tell me,

00:36:13.671 --> 00:36:15.873
share their story about
what they went through

00:36:15.873 --> 00:36:19.944 align:start size:97% position:3%
or their spouse or their
child, or one of their parents

00:36:19.944 --> 00:36:21.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
or somebody, you know,
somebody you love

00:36:21.879 --> 00:36:23.147
has been through this before.

00:36:23.147 --> 00:36:25.249
And it was, it was
pretty incredible.

00:36:25.249 --> 00:36:27.418
- Yeah, did you think
about not coming back

00:36:27.418 --> 00:36:29.453
even once you were
on the other side,

00:36:29.453 --> 00:36:31.389 align:start size:97% position:3%
just because you didn't
want to deal with the stress

00:36:31.389 --> 00:36:33.291
or anything like that?

00:36:33.291 --> 00:36:35.660
- You know, they asked me at
my very first press conference

00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:37.395 line:0%
when I announced this news.

00:36:37.395 --> 00:36:38.729 line:0%
And I'll tell you a funny story.

00:36:38.729 --> 00:36:42.200 line:0%
I had to do a biopsy surgery.

00:36:42.200 --> 00:36:44.268 line:0%
They had to take out a lymph
node out from under my arm.

00:36:44.268 --> 00:36:45.970
And doctor the said, you know,

00:36:45.970 --> 00:36:48.039 align:start size:88% position:13%
"You're gonna to be
fully under anesthesia.

00:36:48.039 --> 00:36:50.741
We're gonna give you some
pretty serious painkillers.

00:36:50.741 --> 00:36:52.243
So you just gotta
go home and rest."

00:36:52.243 --> 00:36:54.445
And I said, "Well, I can't,
I can't go home and rest,

00:36:54.445 --> 00:36:55.880 align:start size:81% position:19%
I gotta go do a
press conference."

00:36:55.880 --> 00:36:59.016 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he says, "Oh, you can't
do a press conference.

00:36:59.016 --> 00:37:00.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, you're
gonna be out of it."

00:37:00.484 --> 00:37:02.053 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I go, "Well, I have
to, because I'm gonna go in

00:37:02.053 --> 00:37:03.955
to start my 24-hour chemo."

00:37:03.955 --> 00:37:05.489
And he goes, "They're not
going to ask you questions

00:37:05.489 --> 00:37:06.424 align:start size:84% position:16%
at this press
conference, are they?"

00:37:06.424 --> 00:37:07.959
And I go, "Yeah, pretty sure

00:37:07.959 --> 00:37:09.594
they're going to
ask me questions."

00:37:09.594 --> 00:37:12.263
So I stood up there and took
questions and they were like,

00:37:12.263 --> 00:37:14.565 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, you know, "Are
you going to resign?"

00:37:14.565 --> 00:37:17.001 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Is the Lieutenant Governor
going to take over?"

00:37:17.001 --> 00:37:18.903 align:start size:88% position:13%
What happens if this and
what happens if that?

00:37:18.903 --> 00:37:20.738
And I was sort of on truth serum

00:37:20.738 --> 00:37:22.673
because I had all
these drugs in me.

00:37:22.673 --> 00:37:24.675
And I just, it was, you know,

00:37:24.675 --> 00:37:26.777
it sounded like I was telling
my family in my living room.

00:37:26.777 --> 00:37:29.447
I just told the whole public
exactly what was going on.

00:37:29.447 --> 00:37:31.782
But I, you know, I never
thought about resigning.

00:37:31.782 --> 00:37:33.417
I'm like everybody else
that went through cancer.

00:37:33.417 --> 00:37:36.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
I still had to try to, you
know, try to do my job.

00:37:36.087 --> 00:37:38.389 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I was working
from the hospital bed.

00:37:38.389 --> 00:37:41.592
You know, they were feeding
me full of so much drugs

00:37:41.592 --> 00:37:44.795 align:start position:0%
and you know, I was doing
steroids and I was up all night.

00:37:44.795 --> 00:37:46.897
So I was actually working
more from the hospital.

00:37:46.897 --> 00:37:49.000
I was texting all night and
telling people, you know,

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:51.135
get this done and get that done.

00:37:51.135 --> 00:37:53.938
- Hmm, well, listen, I know
we're tight on time here

00:37:53.938 --> 00:37:56.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
so my final question,
since you are in Annapolis

00:37:56.774 --> 00:37:58.376
and I mentioned right before

00:37:58.376 --> 00:38:00.177 align:start size:88% position:13%
that I get to
Annapolis pretty often,

00:38:00.177 --> 00:38:02.513
do you know the name
of that Mexican joint

00:38:02.513 --> 00:38:04.715
on Main Street in Annapolis?

00:38:04.715 --> 00:38:06.284
They've got some of
the best margaritas

00:38:06.284 --> 00:38:08.419 align:start size:88% position:13%
and tacos I've ever had,
and I've been trying

00:38:08.419 --> 00:38:11.255 align:start position:0%
to think of the name the
entire time I'm talking to you.

00:38:11.255 --> 00:38:12.690
- That's a really--
- I guarantee one

00:38:12.690 --> 00:38:14.392 align:start size:97% position:3%
of your staff knows--
- Well, one of of the staff,

00:38:14.392 --> 00:38:17.161 align:start size:84% position:16%
what's the best
place on Main Street?

00:38:17.161 --> 00:38:18.362 align:start size:84% position:16%
C'mon.
- The Mexican joint,

00:38:18.362 --> 00:38:19.964
that little Mexican
joint, they do--

00:38:19.964 --> 00:38:21.332
- There's a whole bunch of 'em.

00:38:21.332 --> 00:38:22.867
- They do street tacos.

00:38:22.867 --> 00:38:23.968
Oh, it's just great.

00:38:25.169 --> 00:38:26.704
- I don't know, but
I'll tell you what,

00:38:26.704 --> 00:38:29.073
when you come into Annapolis,
we're going to take you there.

00:38:29.073 --> 00:38:30.708
We're going to find out and
we're gonna get you there.

00:38:30.708 --> 00:38:32.677
You know, we're going
to get some margaritas

00:38:32.677 --> 00:38:34.478 align:start size:81% position:19%
when I see you
here in Annapolis.

